00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.580
What would you give to have perfect eyesight a faster metabolism

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.849
To be a few inches taller or more muscular

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.490
For most of history we've had to deal with the genetic hand we've been dealt and a lot of times

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:18.910
That's not much fun

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:23.729
genetic disorders diseases and inadequacies have plagued our species for thousands of years

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:28.169
But what if we could change all that the answer may be closer than we think?

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:31.889
after a quarter of a million years of slow natural evolution

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:37.529
humanity's on the cusp of having the ability to edit the very building blocks of our being in the past few decades

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:43.140
We've developed tools that help doctors and expecting parents to anticipate any serious health conditions their child may have

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.169
such as hereditary diseases and Down syndrome to

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:52.529
Some people these tests already raise ethical red flags as in many cases especially outside the United States

00:00:52.690 --> 00:01:00.149
The majority of women choose to terminate their pregnancy if the warning signs of Down syndrome are detected as our technology advances

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:06.448
We'll be able to move from detection of these conditions to removal the first step down the road to so-called designer babies

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:13.500
The hope or concern is that soon enough we'll graduate from selecting out the bad genes to selecting in more favorable ones

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.789
This is where many people begin to cry eugenics and advocate extreme caution

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.110
most people would probably agree that

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:26.639
eliminating diseases like Huntington's or quality of life disabilities like poor eyesight would be a good thing and some even suspect that not

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.820
Editing out these genes would constitute an ethical failure in the future

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:37.739
But what about giving your child a leg up on the competition by sprinkling in traits like increased height, or musical proficiency or above-average intelligence?

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:42.569
While the technology to do, this is on its way, we're not there just yet

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:48.360
And it's expected to be very expensive at least at first you might think well if you've got the money for it

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.140
And it's legal

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.829
Why not you're just giving your child the best shot at a successful life

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:57.989
Consider the following scenario a wealthy couple decide they want to enhance their baby's genes

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.819
the average woman typically has 15 eggs extracted for an in-vitro fertilization procedure

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.649
Those 15 potential children will likely differ in IQ by about 20 to 30 points

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:12.599
The parents will be able to select the child or children with superior intelligence to carry to term

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:18.990
Doesn't seem like that big of on the surface, but what happens when the top 10% of society can afford the procedure

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:25.289
But the rest of the population can't now you have a society divided not only by the rapidly increasing wage gap

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:32.700
But also by a new intelligence gap with wealthy children being far more likely to succeed than the 90 percent below them this would further

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:39.030
Stratify our world into the haves and have-nots with modified people only marrying and reproducing with other Modifieds

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:46.320
Thus keeping their gene pool more desirable the solution for this type of situation would be to make genetic modification accessible to all people

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.179
Giving everyone the ability to at least screen out disease and disabilities like poor eyesight

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:56.820
But when we begin to dabble in aesthetic or additive modifications we get to the real dilemma of genetic engineering

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:01.410
Just because we can do something doesn't necessarily mean we should do it

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:08.729
First comes the issue of deciding what this perfect human would look like if we learned anything from Germany during World War two it's that

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.789
When one group dictates what features are best it can cause some serious

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:16.889
problems when parents can start designing their ideal babies like a piece of custom furniture

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:23.309
There's a real danger that people will become competitive adding modifications in a never-ending quest to build the ultimate human child

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.269
Then of course, there's the fact that our technology is rarely infallible

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.700
What happens when a couple orders a six foot four Greek god of a child and he only grows up to be six foot one?

00:03:32.799 --> 00:03:34.299
Could they demand a refund

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:39.599
Would they feel cheated out of the product they ordered how would their child feel about being considered inferior?

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.600
And how would society treat him?

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:42.910
then there's the

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:46.739
international angle China is already known for its questionable social engineering

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:53.459
Practices such as limiting families to one child and taking children away from their parents to be enrolled in Olympic training schools in an attempt

00:03:53.459 --> 00:03:54.630
to build future champions

00:03:54.630 --> 00:04:00.809
It doesn't take much imagination to assume that in a country like that genetic modification would be seen as a very good thing

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:06.959
Parents wouldn't have to worry about their one child being insufficient. They could order a smart strong handsome

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:10.289
Male who would grow up to take care of his parents just like they'd always wanted

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.279
Olympic training schools would be packed with modified children suited to each event

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:19.229
greater lung capacity and endurance for distance runners a smaller leaner built for gymnasts

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:26.429
longer limbs for swimmers when other countries use genetic modification to gain a competitive edge whether in sports or other more serious

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:32.290
Applications, what are the rest of us to do it would be harmful to allow ourselves to be made non-competitive

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.619
But what would it cost to keep up it would be expensive of course?

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:40.660
But extreme genetic engineering may also cost us some of our humanity and of course

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:44.410
There's the potential for unforeseen health consequences to arise and modify children

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.029
DNA is incredibly complex and many traits aren't simply a one gene switch

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:55.750
What happens when we accidentally negate a gene that protects against the disease we have no experience with tinkering with systems

00:04:55.750 --> 00:05:00.130
We don't fully understand is a good way to end up in big trouble at the end of the day

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:05.709
We have some important questions to consider when the technology is widely available will we have an ethical responsibility?

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.649
To modify our children to prevent disease or disability

